raw_knowledge_statement:
Framing the problem as a binary classification we have the negative log-likelihood loss:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:reward_model}
    \mathcal{L}_R(r_{\phi}, \mathcal{D}) = -\mathbb{E}_{(x, y_w, y_l)\sim \mathcal{D}}\bigl[\log \sigma(r_{\phi}(x, y_w)- r_{\phi}(x, y_l))\bigr]
\end{equation}

where $\sigma$ is the logistic function.

The BT model stipulates that the human preference distribution $p^*$ can be written as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:bradley-terry}
    p^*(y_1\succ y_2 \mid x)=\frac{\exp\left(r^*(x, y_1)\right)}{\exp\left(r^*(x, y_1)\right) + \exp\left(r^*(x, y_2)\right)}.
\end{equation}

Following prior works~\citep{jaques2017sequence, jaques2020human}, the optimization is formulated as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:RL}
\max_{\pi_{\theta}}  \mathbb{E}_{x\sim \mathcal{D}, y\sim \pi_{\theta}(y \mid x)}\bigl[r_{\phi}(x, y)\bigr] - \beta\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\bigl[\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\mid \mid \pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\bigr],
\end{equation}

where $\beta$ is a parameter controlling the deviation from the base reference policy $\pi_\text{ref}$, namely the initial SFT model $\pi^\text{SFT}$.

Following prior works~\citep{jaques2017sequence, jaques2020human}, the optimization is formulated as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:RL}
\max_{\pi_{\theta}}  \mathbb{E}_{x\sim \mathcal{D}, y\sim \pi_{\theta}(y \mid x)}\bigl[r_{\phi}(x, y)\bigr] - \beta\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\bigl[\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\mid \mid \pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\bigr],
\end{equation}

where $\beta$ is a parameter controlling the deviation from the base reference policy $\pi_\text{ref}$, namely the initial SFT model $\pi^\text{SFT}$.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model", in the general DPO pipeline, for the given $\pi_\text{ref}$, offline preference dataset $\mathcal{D}$, and desired $\beta$, the language model $\pi_\theta$ is optimized to minimize the objective
(A) \mathcal{L}_\text{reward model}
(B) \mathcal{L}_\text{SFT}
(C) $\mathcal{L}_\text{DPO}$.
(D) \mathcal{L}_\text{KL}
(E) \mathcal{L}_\text{PPO}

According to the paper "Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model", the human preference dataset used for the summarization experiments, on samples from a different, but similarly-trained, SFT model, was gathered by
(A) \citeauthor{bai2022training}
(B) \citeauthor{leandro_von_werra_2023_7790115}
(C) \citeauthor{volske-etal-2017-tl}
(D) \citeauthor{stiennon2022learning}.
(E) \citeauthor{schulman2017proximal}

raw_knowledge_statement:
Specifically, we first take the logarithm of both sides of Eq.~\ref{eq:op_policy} and then with some algebra we obtain:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main_eq}
    r(x,y) =\beta \log \frac{\pi_r(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)} + \beta \log Z(x).
\end{equation}

Specifically, we first take the logarithm of both sides of Eq.~\ref{eq:op_policy} and then with some algebra we obtain:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main_eq}
    r(x,y) =\beta \log \frac{\pi_r(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)} + \beta \log Z(x).
\end{equation}

We define the projection $f$ as  
\begin{equation}
    f(r; \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)(x, y) = r(x, y) - \beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x, y)\right)
\end{equation}

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model", in the optimization objective derived from the control-as-inference framework for the RL fine-tuning step, the complete expression labeled $f(r_{\phi}, \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)$ is
(A) \underbrace{-\beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)}_{\text{soft value function}}
(B) \underbrace{r_{\phi}(x, y) -\beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\theta(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)}_{f(r_{\phi}, \pi_\theta, \beta)}
(C) \underbrace{r_{\phi}(x, y) +\beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)}_{f(r_{\phi}, \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)}
(D) \underbrace{r_{\phi}(x, y) -\beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)}_{f(r_{\phi}, \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)}
(E) \underbrace{r_{\phi}(x, y)-\beta\log\frac{\pi_\theta(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}}_{\text{KL-regularized reward}}

raw_knowledge_statement:
With some algebra this leads to the optimization objective:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:AC}
    \max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)}\bigg[\underbrace{r_{\phi}(x, y) -\beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)}_{f(r_{\phi}, \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)} - \underbrace{\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}}_{\text{KL}}\bigg]
\end{equation}

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model", in the optimization objective in Equation \eqref{eq:AC}, the complete term underbraced as KL is
(A) \beta\log\frac{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}{\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)}
(B) \beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r_{\phi}(x, y)\right)
(C) \beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)}{\pi^*(y\mid x)}
(D) \beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}
(E) \mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}[\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\mid\mid\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)]

